  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CDT, skies are partly to mostly sunny. Some smoke aloft can \nbe seen across the southern two-thirds of the CWA. The near surface \nsmoke has all but moved out of the CWA to the south. Temperatures \nare warming through the 60s into the low 70s this hour (a good\n10 to 20 degrees cooler than this time yesterday). Winds are from\nthe northwest 5 to 15 mph with some occasionally higher gusts. \n\nWith lower heights aloft over the CWA and a decent low level CAA/dry \nair advection sequence ongoing, today and Wednesday continue to look \ncooler than normal for (both high and low) temperatures. Went\naround 3-4 degrees cooler than NBM guidance for tonight's low \ntemperature forecast, based on how the NBM did with last night's\nlow temperature forecast (too warm).\n \nEnsemble clusters analysis and deterministic GSM output continue\nto support a mostly dry extended forecast with a semi-flat \nupper ridge west/upper trof central and east CONUS in place, \nsuch that the steering flow is expected to become west-\nnorthwesterly by Wednesday night through Tuesday of next week. \nExpecting more than one night of low level jet development under\nthis set-up. Once return flow low level moisture regains a \nfoot-hold over this region, will be applying scrutiny to each of\nthese nocturnal low level jet scenarios to see if/when precip \nmention can be inserted into the forecast. There is still quite \na bit of timing/location uncertainty surrounding it (further out\nin time), but guidance is tipping its hat to the potential for,\nat least, one round of rainfall between Sunday afternoon and \nTuesday night. At the moment, there is some halfway decent \nagreement on precipitation chances Sunday evening through Monday\nmorning.\n\nThe low level airmass in place is more than 2 standard deviations \nbelow climo normal (850hpa) right now and is forecast to be more \nthan 1 standard deviation below normal on Wednesday. A slow and \nsteady warm-up into more than 1 standard deviation above climo \nnormal (850hpa) temperatures is forecast into Thursday and Friday, \npeaking over the weekend, before things cool back down to closer to \nclimo normal heading into next week. It still looks like the \n(northwest flow) pattern aloft will support Canada-sourced cold \nfrontal passages every couple of days, helping to stave off another \nheat wave. However, models have been showing some variability in the \nplacement/movement/strength of the western CONUS upper level ridge \nover the past few days. This, ultimately, reflects in the \ntemperature forecast, as a stronger upper ridge further east over \nthe CONUS would mean more heat over this CWA.",
